"Two-in-One" Strategy of o-Amino and Aromatic Nitrogen in Biomimetic Metal-Organic Frameworks for Efficient $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Photoconversion
Visible-light driven photoconversion of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ into energy carriers is highly important to the natural carbon balance and sustainable development. Herein, we demonstrate the biological nucleobase adenine-dependent $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ photoreduction performance in green biomimetic metal-organic frameworks. Photocatalytic results indicate that AD-MOF-2 exhibited a very high HCOOH production rate of $443.2\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathfrak{g}^{-1}\ \mathsf{h}^{-1}$ in pure aqueous solution, which is more than two times higher than that of AD-MOF-1 (179.0 μmol hour−1 ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ ) in acetonitrile solution. Significantly, experimental and theoretical evidences reveal that $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ photoreduction reaction mainly takes place on biological adenine molecules by unique $o$ -amino assisted active aromatic nitrogen atom rather than traditional metal center. This work not only serves as an important case study for the development of green biomimetic photocatalysts used for artificial photosynthesis, but also proposes a new catalytic strategy for efficient $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ photoconversion.
Biological catalysis in nature is an extremely attractive and profound subject due to its eco-friendly, high-efficiency and complicated reaction process.[1] Enzymes as a class of highly sophisticated and powerful biocatlaysts are well-known to play the most crucial role in numerous natural reactions.[2] By some specifically chemical transformations, enzymatic catalysis enables the reaction to occur easily in mild conditions, which is also a longstanding scientific and simulated objective for humans.[3] Of course, the unique catalytic performance of enzymes should be attributed to the synergistic work of multiple components, including active sites, the microenvironment, high substrate affinity, etc.[4] However, some intrinsic drawbacks such as poor thermal stability, pH sensitivity and low tolerance to organic solvents significantly limit enzymes' widespread application.[5] To overcome this dilemma, artificial biomimetic catalysts with enhanced structural robustness, mimicking beneficial features of enzymes, can make catalytic reaction happen in harsh conditions.[6] To date, there are plenty of artificial biomimetic catalysts that have been synthesized and used extensively to some fundamental and complicated catalytic reactions; moreover, sometimes they show much higher catalytic activity and specificity than parent biocatalyst.[7]
In recent years, the increasing anthropogenic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ emission has resulted in serious energy and environmental issues.[8] Substantial research efforts were placed on finding effective approaches to realize the conversion of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ .[9] By imitating the plant photosynthesis, sunlight driven artificial ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction reaction $(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{R R})$ , converting ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into valueadded energy products or chemicals, represents a promising biomimetic conversion pathway.[10] Considering the intrinsically chemical inertness of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecule, the exploration of efficient and stable artificial biocatalysts is regarded as the critical factor of its activation process.[10b] Furthermore, the accomplishment of $C O_{2}R R$ depends largely on the collaborative effect of catalytic active sites, visible-light harvesting, appropriate microenvironment, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ affinity, etc.[10b, 11] Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) as an emerging class of porous materials can exhibit enzyme-like prominent advantages for artificial $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ photoconversion, because they can not only adsorb and activate ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecule through open active moieties but also regulate light collection and channel microenvironment through specifically functionalized organic linkers.[12] However, only limited MOF-based photocatalysts have been reported for $C O_{2}R R$ till now, as most of their structures are easily attacked by $H_{2}O$ molecule and then collapse.[13] Additionally, toxic organic components in MOFs are also not advisable for green synthesis. In this context, increasing structural robustness and environmental friendliness of MOF-based photocatalyst would thus be a more favourable choice for $C O_{2}R R$ .
With these considerations in mind, we focused on the construction of stable and green biomimetic MOF. Herein, two green biomimetic MOFs, $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{H A D})_{2}(\mathsf{A D})_{2}(\mathsf{B A})]\cdot\mathsf{D M F}\cdot2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ (ADMOF-1; HAD $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ adenine and BA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ butanedioic acid) and $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{H A D})_{2}(\mathsf{A D})_{2}(\mathsf{I A})_{2}]$ ·DMF (AD-MOF-2; ${\mathsf{I A}}=$ isobutyric acid), were obtained under solvothermal synthesis method. The auxochromic $-N H_{2}$ and hydrophobic alkyl groups on organic linkers make two MOFs responsive to visible-light and show highly structural aqueous stability.[14] Furthermore, these biomimetic MOFs are more eco-friendly owing to the use of nontoxic biological nucleobase adenine and pharmaceutical ingredients. The photocatalytic characterization reveals that ADMOF-2 showed a very high HCOOH production rate of 443.2 $\upmu\mathrm{m}\mathsf{o l}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ in aqueous solution, which is more than two times higher than that of AD-MOF-1 (179.0 μmol hour−1 ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}.$ ) in acetonitrile solution. Significantly, experiment results and theoretical calculations indicate that $C O_{2}R R$ mainly occurred on biological adenine molecules by $o$ -amino-assisted active aromatic nitrogen atom, which represents a new catalytic strategy for efficient $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ photoreduction.
Single crystal $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction analysis reveals that AD-MOF1 and AD-MOF-2 crystallize in the same $\mu_{1}/\mathsf{a}$ tetragonal space group (Figure S1 and Table S1) and have very similar asymmetric components composed of one independent ${\mathsf{C o}}^{\parallel}$ ions, one $\mathsf{A D}^{-}$ and one half of $\mathsf{B A}^{2-}$ (AD-MOF-1)/one $1A^{-}$ anion (ADMOF-2). The tetrahedral pyramid coordination geometry of Co1 atom in AD-MOF-1 is constructed by bottom two carboxyl O and two N atoms from two ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B}}{\mathsf{A}}$ and two HAD ligands and one axial N1 atom from another HAD ligand (Figure S2). Moreover, two nearby Co1 atoms constitute a paddle-wheel unit (PWU) by sharing two $H_{2}B A$ and two HAD ligands (Figure 1a). Analogously, AD-MOF-2 also exhibits the PWU except linking monocarboxylic HIA instead of dicarboxylic $H_{2}B A$ (Figure 1b). Each HAD ligand links to three Co1 atoms by three aromatic N atoms, the remaining one aromatic N and amino are naked (Figure S2c). The hydrophobic alkyl moieties of HIA or $H_{2}B A$ ligands uniformly point to the cavities of MOF networks (Figure 1c-d). Each PWU in AD-MOF-1 connects six adjacent PWUs by N atoms of HAD or carboxyl groups of $H_{2}B A$ and then generates corner-shared and regular PWU-based tetrahedrons (Figure S3 and 1e). By contrast, every PWU in AD-MOF-2 only communicates with four nearby PWUs by N atoms of HAD ligands. However, because of the extremely similar structural spatial arrangement, AD-MOF-2 still shows corner-shared but distorted PWU-based tetrahedrons (Figure S3 and 1f). If the PWU-based tetrahedron is treated as building block, both the AD-MOFs can form a three dimensional microporous network with classic 6,3 grid-shaped window along crystallographic a axis (Figure 1g-h and S4).
The phase purity and thermal stability of biomimetic AD-MOF1 and AD-MOF-2 were confirmed using well-matched powder Xray diffraction (PXRD) patterns and thermogravimetric analysis, respectively (Figure S5–S7). It has been found that AD-MOF-1 is stable in acetonitrile solution, while AD-MOF-2 remains unchanged in aqueous solutions for several days. Furthermore, their structural robustness can also be maintained under traditional photocatalytic condition for $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ . (Figure S5-6). A broad UV-vis absorption range for AD-MOF-1 and  AD-MOF-2 revealed that they indeed obtain better light-harvesting ability by virtue of auxochromic $(-N H_{2})$ decoration on adenine and ligandto-metal charge transfer (Figure 2a); thus the band gaps of 1.76 (AD-MOF-1) and 1.52 eV (AD-MOF-2) were calculated by the Kubelka–Munk (KM) method (Figure 2b), unveiling their potential to be used as semiconducting photocatalysts.[12a] Also,
Mott–Schottky measurements were performed to determine the LUMO positions of MOFs such that the possible reductive products can be crudely inferred (Figure 2c-d). As we can see, the LUMO locations of MOFs are more negative than the reduction potentials of many photocatalytic products like HCOOH (0.58 V vs. NHE), CO (0.51 V vs. NHE), etc., indicating that the photo-generated electrons can migrate to the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecule for reduction reaction. Additionally, because adenine is considered as an ideal linker having multiple Lewis-basic sites that can interact with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ ,[14b] therefore the volumetric $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption measurements were performed on the activated MOF samples at $298~\mathsf{K}.$ . As shown in Figure S8a, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptakes of biomimetic MOFs were determined to be 52.15 and $41.75\ \mathsf{c m}^{3}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Besides, the porosity of AD-MOF-1 and AD-MOF-2 can also be demonstrated by their ${\sf N}_{2}$ -adsorption isotherms and pore size distribution (Figure S8b).
The visible light-driven $C O_{2}R R$ of the activated AD-MOF-1 (AD-MOF-2) was conducted under pure $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (1.0 atm, 293 K) atmosphere in acetonitrile (aqueous solution) with triisopropanolamine (TIPA) as an electron donor, in the absence of any photosensitizer and noble metal co-catalysts. HCOOH is the only liquid product detected by ion chromatography (IC) (Figure S9), and no other gaseous products including competitive ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution can be observed by gas chromatography (GC) in the whole photoreaction process (Figure S10). Obviously, these two biomimetic MOFs exhibit highly photocatalytic selectivity for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction. As shown in Figure 3a-b, the production of HCOOH for both biomimetic MOFs has a continuous growth as the increased irradiation time. After 4 hours, the maximum HCOOH yield of AD-MOF-1 reaches up to $7.16~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ ( $(179.0~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathfrak{g}^{-1}~\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ . By contrast, ADMOF-2 shows a higher HCOOH production of $22.16~\mathrm{\textmumol}$ (443.2 $\upmu\mathrm{m}\mathsf{o l}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}~\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ after 5 hours, which is among the highest in reported works (Table S2).[12a-c] AD-MOF-2 has higher activity than AD-MOF-1, which is related to less proton amounts in acetonitrile than aqueous solution. After the reaction, the MOFbased photocatalysts were removed from the reaction solutions. The ${\mathsf{C o}}^{\parallel}$ ions leak into the resultant filtrate are determined to be $0.062\%$ (AD-MOF-1) and $0.018\%$ (AD-MOF-2) by Inductivelycoupled plasma analysis. It is worth noting that AD-MOF-2 is the first case of green biomimetic MOF applied in $C O_{2}R R$ with $H_{2}O$ as reaction solvent, it is preferable for the practical feasibility and green synthesis. Furthermore, nontoxic adenine as photosensitizer is, also for the first time, explored to substitute for porphyrin derivative which is the commonly used visible-light harvesting component for $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ .[10c, 12a] To confirm the photocatalytic activity of biomimetic MOFs, a series of deletional control experiments were conducted in the absence of photocatalysts, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ or light illumination, no detectable products were observed by IC and GC in the reaction system (Table S3). The stoichiometric physical mixture effects of raw materials, which is used for the synthesis of AD-MOF-1 and AD-MOF-2, were examined under identical photocatalytic condition. Results revealed that the reductive products are trace amounts of HCOOH and CO (Table S3), corroborating the potential of biomimetic MOFs for photoconverting $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to HCOOH. Also, photoluminescence (PL) emission spectra of AD-MOF-1 and AD-MOF-2 before and after the addition of sacrificial agent were further conducted to investigate the photocatalytic quenching behavior. As shown in Figure S11, the decreased PL intensity of MOF-based photocatalysts suggested that the photo-excited holes in photocatalytic $C O_{2}R R$ process was significantly consumed by TIPA. The photocatalytic stability was evaluated by recycling experiments, in which the photocatalysts nearly maintained their initial activities even after four cycles (Figure 3c-d). The slight decay of the HCOOH production rate is probably due to the mass loss in the recovery process of the used photocatalysts. The resultant filtrates did not show any obvious UV-vis absorption (Figure S12), which excludes
the influence of the decomposed active components from catalysts on the photocatalytic activity. Importantly, no noticeable changes were observed on IR spectra and PXRD patterns performed before and after $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ , confirming the heterogeneous nature of biomimetic AD-MOF-1 and AD-MOF-2 (Figure S13-14). To validate the carbon source origin of the produced HCOOH, the isotopic $^{13}\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ experiment was performed under identical photocatalytic reaction, and the products were identified by $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ NMR spectroscopy. As shown in Figure S15, the $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ NMR spectrum clearly gives the HCOOH signal, which is consistent with the previous works.[12a-c] These results unambiguously demonstrate that biomimetic MOFs are indeed active for converting $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to HCOOH under visible-light irradiation.
The catalytically active sites of biomimetic MOFs for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ -toHCOOH conversion can be identified by their well-defined crystalline structures. It is impractical for the metallic PWU unit in biomimetic MOFs to be active sites because of its saturated coordination sphere. By contrast, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ photoconversion is more likely to occur on adenine moiety with naked aromatic N atom and amino, considering that some nanostructured N-doped materials or N-heterocyclic carbenes have proven to be active in photocatalysis.[15]
In summary, two green biomimetic MOFs were synthesized and used as photocatalysts for $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ . It has been found that AD-MOF-2 exhibited a very high HCOOH production rate in pure aqueous solution. Significantly, $C O_{2}R R$ mainly occurred on adenine molecule by $o$ -amino-assisted active aromatic N atom, which presents a new catalytic strategy for efficient $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ photoreduction. Additionally, because of the employed MOF structures assembled with biological eco-friendly components, we hope this work could provide an important enlightenment to the future development of more stable and green biomimetic MOFs applied in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ photoreduction.
